{"name":"Blues White Shoes","hex":"#99badd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"shoes","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of shoe"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of shoe"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shoes"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Shoes","blocks":[{"text":"White Shoes is the tenth studio album by Emmylou Harris, released in 1983. The album includes a rockish version of \"Diamonds Are a Girl's Best Friend\", a country remake of the Donna Summer hit \"On the Radio\", and a version of Sandy Denny's \"Like an Old Fashioned Waltz\". Both \"In My Dreams\" and \"Pledging My Love\" hit the No. 9 position on the Billboard country music singles chart in 1984."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7f/EmmylouHarrisWhiteShoes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Shoes","wikidata":"Q3567791"},{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues","wikidata":"Q9759"},{"title":"Shoe","blocks":[{"text":"A shoe is an item of footwear normally found in pairs intended to protect and comfort the human foot, usually made in such a way that one is designed to fit the left foot and the other the right foot."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Skor_fr%C3%A5n_1700-_till_1960-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0056302.jpg/330px-Skor_fr%C3%A5n_1700-_till_1960-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0056302.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoe","wikidata":"Q22676"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/MaRainey.jpg/500px-MaRainey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/MaRainey.jpg/500px-MaRainey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American blues singer Ma Rainey (1886–1939), the \"Mother of the Blues\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaRainey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Blues_shuffle_in_E.png/500px-Blues_shuffle_in_E.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Blues_shuffle_in_E.png/500px-Blues_shuffle_in_E.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical blues shuffle rhythm; playⓘ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlues_shuffle_in_E.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Sahel-Sudan.png/500px-Sahel-Sudan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Sahel-Sudan.png/500px-Sahel-Sudan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Sahel (brown) and the Sudan (green)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASahel-Sudan.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Musicologist John Lomax (left) shaking hands with musician \"Uncle\" Rich Brown in Sumterville, Alabama ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn-avery-lom